        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header"><span class="lesson-number">Lesson 6</span><h1>Dependency Inversion Principle</h1><div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>25 min</span><span class="lesson-level advanced">Advanced</span></div></header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Principle</h2>
                    <p><strong>"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions."</strong></p>
                    <p>Also: Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Direct Dependency</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// High-level module depends on low-level module
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly SqlServerDatabase _database = new();  // Concrete!
    private readonly SmtpEmailSender _emailSender = new();  // Concrete!

    public void PlaceOrder(Order order)
    {
        _database.Save(order);
        _emailSender.Send(order.CustomerEmail, "Order placed!");
    }
}

// Can't test without real database and email server!</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>✅ Dependency Inversion</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Abstractions
public interface IDatabase
{
    void Save&lt;T&gt;(T entity);
}

public interface IEmailSender
{
    void Send(string to, string message);
}

// High-level module depends on abstractions
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IDatabase _database;
    private readonly IEmailSender _emailSender;

    public OrderService(IDatabase database, IEmailSender emailSender)
    {
        _database = database;
        _emailSender = emailSender;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder(Order order)
    {
        _database.Save(order);
        _emailSender.Send(order.CustomerEmail, "Order placed!");
    }
}

// Easy to test with mocks!
var mockDb = new Mock&lt;IDatabase&gt;();
var mockEmail = new Mock&lt;IEmailSender&gt;();
var service = new OrderService(mockDb.Object, mockEmail.Object);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Dependency Injection in ASP.NET Core</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Register in Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IDatabase, SqlServerDatabase&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IEmailSender, SmtpEmailSender&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;OrderService&gt;();

// ASP.NET Core injects dependencies automatically
public class OrderController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly OrderService _orderService;

    public OrderController(OrderService orderService)
    {
        _orderService = orderService;
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="key-takeaways">
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concretions.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Use constructor injection for dependencies.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>DI containers wire up dependencies automatically.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Enables testability and flexibility.</span></div>
                    </div>
                </section>
